\section{Rates Turn a Stream Graph into Arithmetic}

StreamIt-style languages expose pipelines, split-joins, feedback loops, and filters. A filter firing consumes, peeks, and produces declared numbers of items. In the following representative syntax, \texttt{pop 1 push 1} states a steady-state rate contract; it is not a performance hint \cite{thies2002streamit}.

\begin{verbatim}
filter Gain(float k) {
  work pop 1 push 1 {
    push(k * pop());
  }
}
pipeline Twice {
  add Gain(2.0);
  add Gain(0.5);
}
\end{verbatim}

For a graph edge from actor \(u\) to actor \(v\), a periodic steady state needs positive repetition counts satisfying \(q_u p_{u,e}=q_v c_{v,e}\), where \(p\) and \(c\) are production and consumption rates. Solving these balance equations does not by itself prove that a schedule avoids deadlock or fits finite buffers. Initial tokens, peek rates, and feedback delay matter. Synchronous dataflow obtains strong scheduling results from fixed rates \cite{lee1987sdf}; dynamic rates require a weaker or runtime-checked plan.

\section{Represent Rates Before Choosing a Schedule}

The graph IR should retain typed channels, actor instances, work regions, rates as exact integers or symbolic affine expressions, peek windows, and initial tokens. A repetition vector and a concrete firing sequence are derived artifacts. Keeping them separate permits another scheduler without reparsing source and prevents a target-specific order from masquerading as semantics. \Cref{fig:visual-368} illustrates a legal balanced schedule for a two-rate chain.

